    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Coral growth is fundamental to the ecological services provided by coral reef ecosystems.  Therefore, understanding the response of coral growth to increased temperature stress under future climate change conditions is critical to predicting future health of coral reefs. Previous studies have found evidence that corals can both adapt and acclimatize to thermal stress, including via symbiont switching. However, few field studies have investigated the effect of thermal resilience on coral growth. To determine the relationship between thermal resilience and coral growth, 92 Acropora hyacinthus corals were randomly chosen in two distinct back reef lagoons in American Samoa with different thermal variability. All corals were genetically sequenced and typed into four previously identified cryptic species. Symbiont type for each colony was determined through gel electrophoresis. The sizes of all colonies were measured from photographs taken of the colonies from August 2010 to December 2014. Mortality, coral inversion, and damage were also recorded. This study is therefore the first baseline assessment of long-term sub-annual growth of Acropora hyacinthus. This is also the first study to document colony growth after coral inversion, whereby A. hyacinthus colonies regrow after being moved into rubble debris field, away from other coral colonies and competition for space and resources. In addition, I found that A. hyacinthus species exhibited two distinct growth patterns, episodic and continuous, suggesting that coral growth is more variable than previously believed. Coral growth rates were significantly different between cryptic species. Species HE, which displays the highest thermal resilience, had significantly lower growth rates than two other species whereas species HD, which has moderate thermal resilience, displayed similar growth rates to the other species. This suggests that certain coral adaptations that confer thermal resilience may come at an energetic cost to the coral. In contrast, coral growth rates were not significantly different between symbiont types. This suggests that harboring thermally resilient symbiont types may be a viable strategy of corals to adapt to thermal stress. These are potentially crucial findings that highlight the need for further research into the energetic tradeoffs of thermal resilience in corals to better understand future impacts of climate change on coral reefs.",
